# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: dribble-eof-prompt-recorded — the prompt pending at EOF lands in the dribble as ";; O: _$"
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-dribble-eof-prompt

* Symptom

Every dribble file that is still open when the session ends gains a
trailing line:

: ;; O: _$

=dribble.issue= states the opposite rule — "prompts are not recorded: a
partial output line still open when an input line completes is prompt
text and is dropped, not emitted."

* Repro (clautolisp 1.8.28)

: printf '(+ 1 1)\n' | clautolisp-sbcl --dribble=/tmp/t.log
: cat /tmp/t.log

Last line is =;; O: _$=. It does *not* appear when the dribble is
closed explicitly with =(clal-dribble)= before exit, because the input
echo has just discarded the pending partial line at that point.

* Cause

=DRIBBLE-STOP= (=clautolisp/tools/clautolisp/source/dribble.lisp=)
calls =(%dribble-terminate-open-line)= with no =:discard=, so a partial
line pending at stop time is emitted rather than dropped. At EOF the
pending partial is the prompt that was printed while waiting for input
that never came.

* Why this is NOT simply "pass :discard t"

The suppression rule elsewhere is evidence-based: the input echo
discards a pending partial *because an input line completed*, which
proves the partial was a prompt. At EOF there is no such proof — a
program ending with =(princ "no trailing newline")= leaves a pending
partial that is genuine output and must be recorded. Discarding
unconditionally at stop would silently drop it.

So the current behaviour is the conservative half of a real tradeoff,
not an oversight: it keeps real output at the cost of one spurious
prompt line. Anyone "fixing" this must preserve unterminated genuine
output.

* Proposal (if judged worth doing at all)

Have the REPL tell the dribble that what it is about to print is a
prompt — e.g. emit prompts through a distinguishable path, or set a
flag around the prompt write — so =DRIBBLE-STOP= can discard a pending
partial *known* to be a prompt and emit anything else. That is the only
way to satisfy both rules at once.

Low priority: one cosmetic line at the end of a file, and the
neighbouring =dribble-stop-announcement-interactor.issue= is in the
same "known, minor, tradeoff" category. WONTFIX is a defensible
outcome; the ticket exists so the next reader does not mistake it for
an unnoticed bug.

* Notes

Found 2026-08-11 while verifying dribble.issue end-to-end for closure.
